From: Ian Blenke <ian@blenke.com>
Date: Wed, 17 Jun 2026 20:12:31 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 02427/11362] [outer-loop] fresh_env A/B sweep over the 10
 unsolved games: 0 unlocks (honest negative)

Operator asked to "loop through the remaining unsolved games and try fresh_env"
(the branch mode that took tu93 L1->L3). New scripts/arc3_fresh_env_sweep.py runs
the SAME bounded best-first search under both replay and fresh_env (generic
adapter-free: rich_action_candidates + grid-hash state key + plain BFS) on all 10
unsolved games, reproduction-gating any advance.

RESULT: 0/10 unlocked. replay == fresh_env == level 0 for every game (avg ~970
states searched). A cheap grid-hash non-idempotent-reset detector false-negatives
on tu93 (the parity only shows at level-completion boundaries), so the sweep
ACTUALLY runs both modes rather than trusting the detector.

CONCLUSION: none of the 10 are blocked by non-idempotent reset -- tu93 was the
only such game. At equal budget fresh_env is provably equivalent to replay here,
so the branch mode is NOT the bottleneck. These games need per-game RE for a
deeper win mechanic (like tn36's program-editor and tu93's frame-based maze each
required), not a branch-mode change. Registry records the result so it is not
blindly re-swept; future effort routes each game to per-game RE.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 4.8 <noreply@anthropic.com>
